1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Hey everyone! Welcome back.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:07,000
So maybe you are not on WordPress.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:11,000
Maybe your website is on Wix, or Blogger,
or a custom-built site that uses React or plain HTML.

4
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:14,000
Good news - VentureX has a REST API
that lets you connect to literally anything.

5
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:18,000
In this video, I am going to show you how the API works,

6
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:21,000
how to create an API token,
and then walk through real integration examples

7
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:24,000
for Wix, Blogger, and custom websites.

8
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:26,000
Let us open up the API docs.

9
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Let us start with how the API works.

10
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:34,000
VentureX uses a standard REST API - that means
you send HTTP requests to specific URLs and get data back.

11
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:38,000
Every request needs two things:
the API URL and an authentication token.

12
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:42,000
The main endpoints you will use are:
POST /api/contacts - this creates a new contact or lead.

13
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:45,000
GET /api/contacts - retrieves your contacts.

14
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:48,000
POST /api/deals - creates a deal or opportunity.

15
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:51,000
GET /api/deals - retrieves your deals.

16
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:54,000
POST /api/webhooks - sets up webhooks
for real-time notifications.

17
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:58,000
Each request needs an Authorization header with your token.

18
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:01,000
The data you send goes in the body as JSON.

19
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:04,000
Let me show you exactly how this works in practice.

20
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Let us create your API token.

21
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:11,000
Go to Settings in VentureX, then API Tokens.

22
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:13,000
Click Create New Token.

23
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:17,000
Give it a descriptive name -
I will use External Integration

24
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:20,000
since this will be used across multiple platforms.

25
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:23,000
Now the important part - permissions.

26
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:27,000
For a basic contact capture setup,
you need: Contacts - Read and Write.

27
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:29,000
That is the minimum.

28
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:33,000
If you want to create deals too,
add Deals - Write.

29
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:37,000
If you want to read product data,
add Products - Read.

30
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:41,000
I will also add Leads - Write, since contacts from external forms
should be tagged as leads.

31
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:43,000
Click Generate.

32
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:46,000
Copy the token right away - you will not see it again.

33
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:48,000
Now let us use it.

34
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Let us start with Wix.

35
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:55,000
Wix has a development environment called Velo
that lets you write custom JavaScript.

36
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:59,000
Open your Wix site in the editor,
click Dev Mode at the top, and turn it on.

37
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:02,000
Now you can add custom code.

38
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:06,000
Create a new backend file - click the plus icon
in the code sidebar, select Backend,

39
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:09,000
name it venturex.jsw.

40
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:12,000
This is where your API calls live.

41
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:16,000
Now I will paste in a function
to send contacts to VentureX.

42
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:22,000
Here is the code: a function that takes contactData
and sends a POST request to your API endpoint.

43
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:26,000
It sets the Content-Type to application/json
and adds your Authorization Bearer token in the headers.

44
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:30,000
The body is the contactData converted to JSON,
and it returns the response as JSON.

45
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:34,000
Now in your frontend page code,
call this function when a form is submitted.

46
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:38,000
Let me show you - find your form submit handler,

47
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:44,000
and call sendToVenturex with the name, email, phone,
and source fields from your form inputs.

48
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:47,000
That is it. When someone fills out your Wix form,
it sends the data straight to VentureX.

49
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:51,000
Test it by submitting the form
and checking your VentureX contacts.

50
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Now Blogger.

51
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:57,000
Blogger is simpler - we will use a JavaScript gadget.

52
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:00,000
Log into Blogger, go to Layout.

53
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:03,000
Click Add a Gadget and choose HTML/JavaScript.

54
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:05,000
Title it Contact Us.

55
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:09,000
Now paste in this code - it is a simple form
with JavaScript that sends data to VentureX

56
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:11,000
when submitted.

57
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:15,000
The form has name, email, and message fields.

58
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:19,000
The script captures the form data on submit,
packages it as JSON,

59
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:23,000
and sends a POST request to your VentureX API endpoint
using the fetch API.

60
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:26,000
Make sure to replace the API URL and token with your own.

61
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:28,000
Click Save, then View Blog.

62
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:31,000
You will see your form on the page.

63
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:34,000
Fill it in and submit - check VentureX and the contact appears.

64
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:38,000
One thing to note - Blogger Content Security Policy
might block external requests.

65
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:42,000
If that happens, you will need to use a proxy service
or host a small Cloudflare Worker

66
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:45,000
that forwards the request.
I will link a tutorial for that in the description.

67
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Got a custom website? Here is how to integrate
with any tech stack.

68
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:52,000
Let me show you three methods.

69
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:56,000
Method one - JavaScript fetch,
for any modern website.

70
00:03:56,200 --> 00:04:00,000
Add this to your contact form submit handler:
a fetch call to your API contacts endpoint

71
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:04,000
with POST method, JSON headers,
your Bearer token in Authorization,

72
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:08,000
and the form data as JSON.stringify in the body.

73
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:12,000
Method two - PHP cURL,
for server-side integration.

74
00:04:12,200 --> 00:04:16,000
This is more secure because your API token
stays on the server.

75
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:20,000
Initialize curl with your API URL,
set POST to true,

76
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:24,000
set the HTTP headers with Content-Type and Authorization,

77
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:28,000
set POSTFIELDS to json_encode of your form data,
set_RETURNTRANSFER to true, and execute.

78
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:32,000
Method three - Python requests,
for data pipelines or scripts.

79
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:36,000
Just use requests.post with your API URL,
json payload, and headers with your Bearer token.

80
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:39,000
Any of these approaches work.
Pick the one that matches your stack.

81
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Webhooks let VentureX push data to YOUR systems
in real-time.

82
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:48,000
Say you want to get a Slack notification
every time a new deal is won,

83
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:51,000
or push contacts to a Google Sheet.

84
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:54,000
In VentureX, go to Settings, then Webhooks.

85
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:56,000
Click Create Webhook.

86
00:04:56,200 --> 00:04:59,000
Give it a name - Slack Notification.

87
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:02,000
Set the URL - that is where VentureX will send the data.

88
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:06,000
For Slack, you would use an incoming webhook URL
from your Slack workspace.

89
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:10,000
Choose the events that trigger it -
I will select Deal Won and New Contact.

90
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:13,000
You can also set a secret key
for verifying the webhook payload.

91
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:15,000
Save it.

92
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:18,000
Now test - create a deal and mark it as won.

93
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:21,000
Check Slack - you should see the notification arrive instantly.

94
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:25,000
You can create multiple webhooks for different events
and different destinations.

95
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:28,000
This is how you build real-time workflows
without any polling or scheduled tasks.

96
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Quick but important section on security.

97
00:05:32,200 --> 00:05:36,000
If you are calling the API from a browser-based app -
like Wix, Blogger, or a custom React site -

98
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:39,000
you might run into CORS errors.

99
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:42,000
That is the browser blocking cross-origin requests
for security.

100
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:46,000
To fix this, VentureX needs to include
the right CORS headers.

101
00:05:46,200 --> 00:05:50,000
In your Laravel config, open config/cors.php.

102
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:54,000
Set allowed_origins to include your website domain -
for example, your Wix site URL.

103
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:58,000
If you are developing locally,
add http://localhost:3000.

104
00:05:58,200 --> 00:06:01,000
Set supports_credentials to true
if you are using cookies.

105
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:05,000
A few other security tips:
never put your API token in client-side JavaScript

106
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:08,000
that is visible to everyone.

107
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:12,000
For public-facing forms, use a backend proxy
or serverless function.

108
00:06:12,200 --> 00:06:15,000
Always use HTTPS - never send API tokens
over plain HTTP.

109
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:19,000
And rotate your tokens periodically -
if a token is compromised, regenerate it.

110
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:22,000
These are not just best practices -
they protect your customers data.

111
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
And there you have it - Wix, Blogger, custom sites,
all connected to VentureX.

112
00:06:26,200 --> 00:06:30,000
The REST API is your Swiss Army knife.
Any platform that can make an HTTP request

113
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:33,000
can send data to your CRM.

114
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:37,000
In the next video, we will cover backup, restore,
and troubleshooting

115
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:40,000
- keeping your VentureX installation
safe and running smooth.

116
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:42,000
See you there!
